The code statements that lead to the bug are:
list_for_each_entry(ctx, &local->chanctx_list, list) {
ieee80211_dfs_cac_cancel(local, ctx);
Reason:
The loop uses `list_for_each_entry` which does not hold a safe pointer to the next entry. Inside the loop, `ieee80211_dfs_cac_cancel` can free and remove the current `ctx` from the list, causing the iterator to access freed memory when moving to the next entry (slab-use-after-free).

The code statements that fix the bug are:
struct ieee80211_chanctx *ctx, *tmp;
list_for_each_entry_safe(ctx, tmp, &local->chanctx_list, list) {
Reason:
`list_for_each_entry_safe` stores the next entry in `tmp` before the loop body executes, allowing safe removal of the current entry. Even if `ieee80211_dfs_cac_cancel` frees `ctx`, the iteration continues correctly using `tmp`, preventing use-after-free.

